{"name":"Buddha’s Love Handles","hex":"#ffbb33","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"buddha","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Buddha.","Marijuana."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/buddha"},{"term":"love","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deep caring for the existence of another.","Strong affection."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To have a strong affection for (someone or something).","To need, thrive on."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/love"},{"term":"handles","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of handle"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of handle"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/handles"}],"entries":[{"title":"Love Handles (game show)","blocks":[{"text":"Love Handles was a Canadian television game show produced by Blair Murdoch from 1996 to 1998 on the Global Television Network, and hosted by Stu Jeffries. The show's announcer was David Kaye."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Love_Handles_(game_show)","wikidata":"Q6690594"},{"title":"Love","blocks":[{"text":"Love encompasses a range of strong and positive emotional and mental states, from the most sublime virtue or good habit, the deepest interpersonal affection, to the simplest pleasure. An example of this range of meanings is that the love of a mother differs from the love of a spouse, which differs from the love for food. Most commonly, love refers to a feeling of strong attraction and emotional attachment."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Love","wikidata":"Q316"},{"title":"Handle","blocks":[{"text":"A handle is a part of, or an attachment to, an object that allows it to be grasped and manipulated by hand. The design of each type of handle involves substantial ergonomic issues, even where these are dealt with intuitively or by following tradition. Handles for tools are an important part of their function, enabling the user to exploit the tools to maximum effect. Package handles allow for convenient carrying of packages."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Tsuka.JPG/330px-Tsuka.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Handle","wikidata":"Q200266"},{"title":"The Buddha","blocks":[{"text":"Siddhartha Gautama, most commonly referred to as the Buddha, was a wandering religious teacher who lived in the eastern Indo-Gangetic Plains during the 6th or 5th century BCE and founded Buddhism. According to Buddhist legends, he was born in Lumbini, in what is now Nepal, to royal parents of the Shakya clan, but renounced his home life to live as a wandering ascetic. After leading a life of mendicancy, asceticism, and meditation, he attained nirvana at Bodh Gaya in what is now Bihar, India. The Buddha then wandered through the lower Indo-Gangetic Plain, teaching and building a monastic order (sangha). Buddhist tradition holds he died in Kushinagar and reached parinirvana."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Buddha_in_Sarnath_Museum_%28Dhammajak_Mutra%29.jpg/330px-Buddha_in_Sarnath_Museum_%28Dhammajak_Mutra%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Buddha","wikidata":"Q9441"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg/500px-Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg/1280px-Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg/500px-Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Frate